1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,265
Greetings, aliens and earthlings!

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:13,866
This is not an episode of Earthlings 101, your crash course for alien visitors, but a series of three shorter videos about something that seems to confuse earthlings and aliens alike: The shape of the universe.

3
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:45,487
Most earthlings and aliens don't understand space.

4
00:00:45,927 --> 00:00:47,367
It's not what they think it is.

5
00:00:47,807 --> 00:00:50,530
People assume that the universe is infinite and flat.

6
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:57,267
But actually, from a non-Euclidean, non-local viewpoint, it's more like a big ball of wibbly wobbly, spacey wacey stuff.

7
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:01,570
And the shape of this big ball - that's what this three-part series is all about.

8
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:06,990
When we talk about "shape", we are basically talking about two things: Topology and Geometry.

9
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:11,875
Geometry is all about distances, angles, areas and so on.

10
00:01:12,439 --> 00:01:17,620
Topology doesn't care about exact angles and distances - it's about which zones are next to each other.

11
00:01:18,042 --> 00:01:26,929
For example, when you deform a square, you change the geometry, but not the topology, as long as you don't rip it apart, create holes, or glue parts together.

12
00:01:26,929 --> 00:01:34,576
For the sake of simplicity, let's neglect the expansion of the universe and only consider a "snapshot" of the universe, i.e. a slice of space time.

13
00:01:35,297 --> 00:01:39,197
Unfortunately, I don't know with certitude what the actual shape of this slice is.

14
00:01:39,557 --> 00:01:44,542
All research on cosmo-topology is classified as "secret of defense" by the Central Galactic Bureaucracy.

15
00:01:44,884 --> 00:01:49,217
So, I can only discuss hypotheses - different possible models of the universe.

16
00:01:49,622 --> 00:01:54,055
Basically there are three kinds of models: Spherical, flat, and hyperbolical.

17
00:01:54,336 --> 00:01:56,266
Today we will talk about flat models.

18
00:01:56,693 --> 00:02:00,413
Flat or not - there are a lot of misconceptions about the universe around.

19
00:02:09,393 --> 00:02:10,493
No, Hank!

20
00:02:10,873 --> 00:02:15,293
A finite universe, and a universe with an edge, are two wholly different things.

21
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:25,413
No, Gorf.

22
00:02:25,573 --> 00:02:29,470
"Curved space" doesn't mean that the universe curves through an embedding hyperspace.

23
00:02:29,470 --> 00:02:33,070
It only means that the laws of geometry are not what you would expect.

24
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:42,848
It doesn't expand into anything - it just expands.

25
00:02:59,953 --> 00:03:00,845
No, Derek.

26
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:06,186
A flat universe isn't necessarily infinite, and can contain straight lines that lead back to their origin.

27
00:03:15,469 --> 00:03:16,669
To be honest...

28
00:03:17,089 --> 00:03:18,269
That's just stupid.

29
00:03:19,589 --> 00:03:22,669
So, what does "finite universe" mean, anyway?

30
00:03:22,949 --> 00:03:25,282
It means that the universe has finite volume.

31
00:03:25,282 --> 00:03:30,060
In other words, you'd need only a finite amount of hydric acid to inundate the whole universe.

32
00:03:31,128 --> 00:03:39,248
"No edge", on the other hand, means that when you explore the universe, you won't suddenly run into a wall of nothingness - an edge.

33
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:41,075
But isn't that the same?

34
00:03:41,409 --> 00:03:47,188
When there is no edge, you can just fly on and on, and boldly go where no man or alien has gone before.

35
00:03:47,554 --> 00:03:52,233
And when you do so, you see more and more volume, so the total volume must be infinite...

36
00:03:52,596 --> 00:03:53,036
Right?

37
00:03:55,836 --> 00:03:56,357
Wrong.

38
00:03:56,899 --> 00:04:03,199
Because when you fly on long enough, you might eventually find yourself in known space - you might even return to your home planet.

39
00:04:03,599 --> 00:04:06,069
In other words, space may bend into itself.

40
00:04:06,777 --> 00:04:10,137
To understand this, let's look what happens in two dimensions.

41
00:04:10,817 --> 00:04:16,180
Like many alien races, Earthlings have gone through a stage in history when they wondered about the shape of their planet's surface.

42
00:04:16,683 --> 00:04:20,483
Some imagined an infinite surface, whereas others believed the surface had an edge.

43
00:04:21,563 --> 00:04:23,640
Eventually they found out that both were wrong.

44
00:04:24,035 --> 00:04:32,375
There is no edge, but the surface is finite: When you sail away into one direction and keep sailing in a straight line, you will eventually come back to where you came from.

45
00:04:33,635 --> 00:04:36,435
The reason is, of course, that earth is a sphere.

46
00:04:37,115 --> 00:04:41,013
Finite surfaces without edge can easily be understood by 3 dimensional beings.

47
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:46,000
But how would you explain them to two-dimensional creatures, like the Flatsnails from Regulus three?

48
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:51,710
Flatsnails can only think in two dimensions, but they have a highly developed portal technology.

49
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:59,121
A Flatsnail portal alters the topology of a surface by glueing different parts of a surface together - for example two opposing sides of a square.

50
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:04,821
When a Flatsnail leaves the rectangle on one side, she enters on the other side - like in a video game.

51
00:05:05,701 --> 00:05:07,961
Topologically, this is the same thing as a cylinder.

52
00:05:08,461 --> 00:05:10,670
Let's call it a video game cylinder.

53
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:13,550
What would a video game cylinder look like from the interior?

54
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:19,984
Flatsnails can't see very far, but if they could, they would see an endless series of images of the same space.

55
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:23,344
A bit like what you see when you stand between two parallel mirrors.

56
00:05:24,024 --> 00:05:27,644
What happens when you glue the other two sides of the square together, as well?

57
00:05:27,964 --> 00:05:30,776
Now you have the loopy video game effect in two directions.

58
00:05:31,417 --> 00:05:35,064
A farsighted Flatsnail inside would see her own image repeated in a grid pattern.

59
00:05:35,750 --> 00:05:38,753
Topologically, this is a torus - the surface of a donut.

60
00:05:39,261 --> 00:05:42,961
So, let's call the double-loopy rectangle "video game torus".

61
00:05:43,188 --> 00:05:47,656
The important point is that you don't need the third dimension to define a cylinder or a torus.

62
00:05:47,999 --> 00:05:50,739
All you need is portals, to glue the edges together.

63
00:05:50,979 --> 00:05:57,205
And once the glueing is done, you can forget about the portals and just enjoy your seamless yet loopy video game environment.

64
00:05:57,506 --> 00:06:05,106
A Flatsnail that grew up on this torus would be used to the fact that she lives on a flat, finite, somewhat loopy surface, without edge.

65
00:06:05,566 --> 00:06:09,965
No need for portals or bendy space, not to mention this esoterical "third dimension".

66
00:06:10,983 --> 00:06:13,128
But what about the universe we live in ?

67
00:06:13,443 --> 00:06:17,129
Is it so unthinkable that our universe is similar - only in three dimensions?

68
00:06:17,514 --> 00:06:18,461
Why shouldn't it?

69
00:06:18,729 --> 00:06:25,259
Is there any reason why the universe has to be either a Euclidean space, or embedded in a higher dimensional Euclidean hyperspace?

70
00:06:25,604 --> 00:06:26,970
But back to two dimensions.

71
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:30,414
Let's toy around some more with surfaces and portals.

72
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:35,700
For example, what happens when we glue together two sides of a rectangle, but with flipped directions?

73
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:40,837
Well, we get a Moebius strip - a surface where each round trip makes you flip orientation.

74
00:06:41,223 --> 00:06:44,443
When you glue the other pair without flipping, you get a Klein bottle.

75
00:06:45,563 --> 00:06:49,203
When you flip both pairs, you get something called Projective Plane.

76
00:06:50,063 --> 00:06:52,743
Now, we are not limited to rectangles, though.

77
00:06:53,003 --> 00:06:57,163
We can, for example, take a parallelogram and glue opposing edges together.

78
00:06:57,783 --> 00:07:01,452
Geometrically this is a skewed torus, topologically it's the same thing.

79
00:07:01,769 --> 00:07:05,509
A Flatsnail sitting inside would see herself repeated in a skewed grid pattern.

80
00:07:06,229 --> 00:07:10,123
What happens if you take a hexagon and glue opposing sides together without flipping?

81
00:07:10,468 --> 00:07:14,128
Well, a flatsnail inside would see herself in the same pattern as before.

82
00:07:14,528 --> 00:07:17,941
So this is just another way to construct the same skewed torus.

83
00:07:18,543 --> 00:07:20,783
Let's go back to the video game torus.

84
00:07:21,143 --> 00:07:24,010
Can't we do a similar construction in three-dimensional space?

85
00:07:24,317 --> 00:07:25,437
Of course we can.

86
00:07:25,777 --> 00:07:31,411
All we have to do is take a cuboid in space, and link opposing sides with portals without twisting anything.

87
00:07:31,713 --> 00:07:40,667
If some spacefarer happens to be trapped inside, he will find himself in a three dimensional video game space where he comes back to his origin when he flies long enough along one of the axis.

88
00:07:41,372 --> 00:07:46,652
He won't even notice the portals - he would just experience a universe that somehow repeats itself.

89
00:07:46,652 --> 00:07:47,912
This is called a hyper-torus.

90
00:07:49,772 --> 00:07:57,212
Just like our video game torus, the hypertorus is "flat", which means that the rules of school geometry still hold for very large objects.

91
00:07:57,712 --> 00:08:00,612
In part 2 we will define more precisely what this means.

92
00:08:01,332 --> 00:08:02,990
Did you see what we just did?

93
00:08:03,154 --> 00:08:06,905
We constructed a flat universe, with finite volume, but no edge.

94
00:08:07,328 --> 00:08:09,421
Something Hank and Derek thought was impossible.

95
00:08:09,902 --> 00:08:17,622
Such a universe looks exactly like infinite flat three-dimensional space, except that it repeats itself like a hotel wallpaper - in three directions.

96
00:08:17,982 --> 00:08:22,366
Some earthling scientists think that the hyper-torus is a possible shape of the universe.

97
00:08:25,408 --> 00:08:27,148
Yes Earthling, no edge.

98
00:08:28,408 --> 00:08:31,268
But the hypertorus is not the only flat finite model.

99
00:08:31,688 --> 00:08:35,693
For example, you can obtain another model by turning the top portal by 90°.

100
00:08:36,095 --> 00:08:38,475
This is called the "quarter-turn manifold".

101
00:08:38,855 --> 00:08:44,055
A third variant is the "half-turn manifold", obtained by turning the top portal by 180°.

102
00:08:45,255 --> 00:08:47,985
Remember how we constructed a torus with a honeycomb pattern?

103
00:08:48,252 --> 00:08:50,959
You can do the same with a hexagonal prism to get the hypertorus.

104
00:08:51,723 --> 00:09:00,663
Now, when you rotate the top gate by 60° to get the "one-sixth turn manifold", or by 120° to obtain the "one-third turn manifold".

105
00:09:01,023 --> 00:09:07,233
There are overall 10 models for a finite closed flat universe - 6 orientable and 4 non-orientable ones.

106
00:09:07,555 --> 00:09:09,286
What does "non-orientable" mean?

107
00:09:09,536 --> 00:09:14,501
It means that the universe is like a moebius strip: When you make a round trip, you might come home mirror-inverted.

108
00:09:15,401 --> 00:09:17,329
This is called an Alice Universe.

109
00:09:17,329 --> 00:09:19,209
For you, your planet will be mirror-inverted.

110
00:09:20,289 --> 00:09:26,049
Also, as electrical charge is a matter of orientation, the planet and everything on it will consist of antimatter.

111
00:09:28,989 --> 00:09:33,111
But the ten finite, closed, flat models are not the only possibilities.

112
00:09:33,417 --> 00:09:39,272
In part two and three we will see other, curved models for the universe, like the Hyper-sphere or the Poincaré space.

113
00:09:39,573 --> 00:09:43,165
We will also talk about evidence that may help finding out which model is correct.

114
00:09:43,652 --> 00:09:46,772
Of course, the crash course for alien visitors continues.

115
00:09:46,772 --> 00:09:54,397
What do you think: Should I alternate between the Crash Course and No Edge episodes, or rather finish this mini-series and then continue the crash course?

116
00:09:54,719 --> 00:09:59,779
Tell me your preference in the comments below, or on my Twitter account, or my Facebook page.

117
00:10:00,219 --> 00:10:01,219
Thanks for watching.

118
00:10:01,698 --> 00:10:09,480
Like if you enjoyed it, share if you really enjoyed it, subscribe if you don't want to miss my next videos, and as always: Don't forget to be alien.

